 C Programming Notes — Loops, Return, and Switch-Case

 1. Pattern Program (Right Triangle of *)


int main()
{
    int i, j, k;
    printf("Enter number of rows: ");
    scanf("%d", &r);

    for (i = 0; i < r; i++)
    {
        for (j = 0; j < r - 1 - i; j++)
            printf(" ");
        for (k = 0; k <= i; k++)
            printf("* ");
        printf("\n");
    }
    return 0;
}



 2. While Loop

Syntax:

while (exp)
{
    st1;
    st2;
    ...
}

- The arrow flow: while(exp) → condition check → body executes if true.
- while() with no condition → Error — a 'while' can't be empty.

Example:

int i = 0;
while (i < 5)
{
    printf("Hello\n");
    i++;
}
return 0;

Output:

Hello
Hello
Hello
Hello
Hello


### Dummy While Loop (loop ends at the ;)


int i = 0;
while (i++ <= 2);      // dummy loop — loop ends here (note the semicolon)
printf("%d\n", i);     // not part of the loop
return 0;


Trace — dummy while loop (i++, post-increment used in check):
| i (start) | check i <= 2 | i (after) |

| 0         | 0<=2 ✓      | 1 |
| 1         | 1<=2 ✓      | 2 |
| 2         | 2<=2 ✓      | 3 |
| 3         | 3<=2 ✗      | → exits, final i = 4|


Trace — normal while loop (++i, pre-increment, body runs):
| i (start) | check i <= 2 | body runs | i (after) |

| 0        | 0<=2 ✓       | prints 1 | 1 |
| 1        | 1<=2 ✓       | prints 2 | 2 |
| 2        | 2<=2 ✓       | prints 3 | 3 |
| 3        | 3<=2 ✗       | exits | 4 |

Key distinction:
- Dummy while loop → condition itself does the work (via i++), body is empty (just ;).
- Normal while loop → condition just checks; the body does the work.


