 3. for Loop vs while Loop

Difference between for loop and while loop:

| For Loop -
| At a time of writing the program, if we found: initialization, condition, increment/decrement → fixed 
| We need to select 'for' loop.
| it can be empty. ()


| While Loop -
| At a time of writing the program, if we found: initialization, condition, increment/decrement → not fixed 
| May change, so we need to select 'while' loop.
| it cannot be empty () loop


Example:

for (i = 0; i = 2, i <= 4; i++)     // fixed iterations
while (i = 0; i = 2, i <= 4, i++)   // syntax used when it's not fixed
    printf("%d\n", i);




 4. 'return' Statement

- return is applicable within a function only.
- When return executes in a program, in which function it executes, the control flow of that function will stop.
- return can be used in two different ways:
  1. Control return: return;
  2. Value return: return 0; or return 1, 2, ...

Syntax:

return;

Return is one of the non-iterative conditional CS (control statements).

Example:

void main()
{
    printf("Hello ");
    printf("World.");
    return 0;
    printf("Bye 1.");   // unreachable — never executes
}

Output: Hello World.



 5. Switch-Case

- switch case is one of the non-iterative conditional control statements.
- It is used for menu-based tasks.

Syntax:

switch (exp)
{
    case label1: st1;
                 break;
    case label2: st2;
                 break;
    case label3: st3;
                 break;
    default: st4;
}


Rules:
- 'exp' result must be of int type.
- Case label must be an int family constant.
- 'break' and 'default' are - not mandatory in switch.
- switch() cannot be empty — error.
- If 'break' is not used, all case bodies below the matching case execute (fall-through) until a break is hit.
- 'default' can be placed anywhere — it doesn't have to be last. It will execute if no case matches.

Example:

#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
    int n;
    printf("Enter n value: \n");
    scanf("%d\n", &n);

    switch (n)
    {
        case 1: printf("one\n");
                break;
        case 2: printf("two\n");
                break;
        case 3: printf("three\n");
                break;
        default: printf("not one two three\n");
    }
    return 0;
}


 Switch Trace Examples

switch (1 + 0)   // printf("one");   // Valid

case 'a': printf("one"); break;   // char 'a' = 97 → valid, works like int
case 9.7: printf("three"); break;   // Error — case label must be int, not float



switch (n)
{
    case 1+0: printf("one");
    break;
    case 'p': printf("two");
    break;
    case 3: printf("three");
    break;
}

Ans: if n = 4 → % p = not one two three

- If 'break' is not used, control falls through and executes all cases below the matching one, until a 'break' is found or the switch ends.
- If we write duplicate case labels, it gives an error — no repetition of case label allowed.
- 'default' can be written anywhere inside switch, but will execute only if none of the cases match.


 Example: Vowel Checker

// WAP to scan one char from user and print char is vowel or not.
#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
    char ch;
    printf("Enter char: \n");
    scanf("%c", &ch);

    switch (ch)
    {
        case 'a':
        case 'e':
        case 'i':
        case 'o':
        case 'u':
            printf("lower case vowel\n");
            break;

        case 'A':
        case 'E':
        case 'I':
        case 'O':
        case 'U':
            printf("upper case vowel\n");
            break;

        default: printf("not vowel\n");
    }
}


 Example: Basic Calculator Using Switch-Case

// WAP program for basic Calculatore functions

#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
    int n1, n2;
    printf("Enter n1 & n2 value: \n");
    scanf("%d %d\n", &n1, &n2);

    char op;
    printf("press + for add\n"
           "press - for sub\n"
           "press * for mul\n"
           "press / for div\n");
    scanf(" %c", &op);   // space before %c to skip whitespace/newline

    switch (op)
    {
        case '+': printf("add: %d\n", n1 + n2);
                  break;
        case '-': printf("sub: %d\n", n1 - n2);
                  break;
        case '*': printf("mul: %d\n", n1 * n2);
                  break;
        case '/': printf("div: %d\n", n1 / n2);
                  break;
        default:  printf("invalid option\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
